    "title": "Federal Court sentences Daniel Callihan to life in prison - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Rick Gogreve ,\n1 hours ago\nNEW ORLEANS ( WGNO ) — 38-year-old Daniel Callihan was handed two more consecutive life sentences after his sentencing in federal court.\nHe had already received life sentences for pleading guilty to murder and kidnapping in both the Louisiana and Mississippi state cases.\n“Well, it was expected. You know, the family had to get through it. This is not their first time to sit through this, a sentencing. And, it’s been a long run along ordeal for them,” Tangipahoa District Attorney Scott Perrilloux said.\nSuspect wanted in connection with Gretna homicide During the federal sentence hearing, a video message was played of Callihan where he said he had hurt a “wonderful friend” and “two very innocent kids.”\nHe also said how sorry he was for what he had done and that there was nothing that he could do to take it back.\n“I think everyone who has any familiarity with this case knows that the man that stood in front of the court today is a different man than the judge was talking about today,” Callihan’s lawyer Kerry Cuccia said.\nAfter Callihan’s video, Callie Burnett’s sister spoke, sharing stories of the mother and daughter Callihan killed.\n“Just unimaginable how you get through something like that and live with it and then have to go into a courtroom and express how you feel about certain things,” Perilloux said.\nThis sentencing marked the end of Callihan’s court proceedings, and he will spend the rest of his life at Angola.\n“I don’t think there’s anything closure wise, but to get to another step where you don’t have to… this will be their last trip to this building for this case. We still have a little more to do, but, overall, they were pleased with what’s happened, and, of course, ready and try to move on with their lives,” Perrilloux said.\nStay up to date with the latest news, weather and sports by downloading the WGNO app on the Apple or Google Play stores and by subscribing to the WGNO newsletter .\nLatest Posts Southeastern looks to keep FCS playoff hopes alive in 36th edition of the River Bell Classic Another foggy morning on the way Jokic’s triple-double and Watson’s career-high 32 points push the Nuggets past the Pelicans, 125-118 Federal Court sentences Daniel Callihan to life in prison Trump signs Epstein bill into law, releasing files from DOJ Copyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WGNO.\n1",
